The array can be viewed as two parts: the values that move to the front and the values that stay after them. Reversing the whole array first puts both parts in reverse order. Reversing each part again restores their order.

Dry run for `[1,2,3,4,5,6,7]` and `k = 3`:

- Full reverse: `[7,6,5,4,3,2,1]`
- Reverse the first 3: `[5,6,7,4,3,2,1]`
- Reverse the rest: `[5,6,7,1,2,3,4]`

All changes happen inside the given vector. The `k %= n` step also handles values of `k` larger than the array length.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void rotateArray(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n == 0) {
            return;
        }

        k %= n;
        reverse(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        reverse(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k);
        reverse(nums.begin() + k, nums.end());
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input vector.

Kept the operation very direct here. After reducing `k`, the code moves the last value to the front one step at a time. The inner loop shifts the other values to the right before placing the saved value at index `0`.

For example, `[1,2,3,4,5]` with `k = 2` becomes `[5,1,2,3,4]` after one step, then `[4,5,1,2,3]` after the second. The array is changed in place, but this is slower than the one-pass methods when `k` is large.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void rotateArray(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n == 0) {
            return;
        }

        k %= n;

        for (int step = 0; step < k; step++) {
            int last = nums[n - 1];

            for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--) {
                nums[i] = nums[i - 1];
            }

            nums[0] = last;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * k)` after reducing `k`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Each value can be sent straight to its final position instead of moving the array step by step. An item at index `i` moves to `(i + k) % n`, so a second array keeps the original values while the positions are filled.

This is easy to check because no value is overwritten before it is read. The trade-off is the extra array. An empty input is also ignored before using the remainder operation.

```java
class Solution {
    public void rotateArray(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return;
        }

        k %= n;
        int[] rotated = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int newIndex = (i + k) % n;
            rotated[newIndex] = nums[i];
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = rotated[i];
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the second array.

The direct movement rule also works without a second array. Each position sends its value to `(index + k) % n`, and the displaced value continues to the next position. Some cycles may not cover the whole array, so the code starts another cycle until every element has been moved.

The greatest common divisor tells how many separate cycles are needed. For `n = 6` and `k = 2`, for example, there are two cycles. The input array is modified in place.

```java
class Solution {
    public void rotateArray(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return;
        }

        k %= n;
        if (k == 0) {
            return;
        }

        int cycles = gcd(n, k);
        int moved = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < cycles; start++) {
            int current = start;
            int value = nums[current];

            do {
                int next = (current + k) % n;
                int saved = nums[next];
                nums[next] = value;
                value = saved;
                current = next;
                moved++;
            } while (current != start);
        }
    }

    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int remainder = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = remainder;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every element is moved once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

